\section*{Preface}
This document serves as an extensive technical guide for the DNA methylation analysis pipeline implemented by Group 4. It integrates theoretical foundations, detailed R code explanations, best practices, and troubleshooting tips to empower rigorous and reproducible Illumina HumanMethylation450K data analysis.

\section{Glossary of Key Terms}

\begin{tcolorbox}[
    breakable,
    colback=white,
    colframe=black!80,
    boxrule=0.7pt,
    arc=4pt,
    left=6pt,
    right=6pt,
    top=6pt,
    bottom=6pt,
    fonttitle=\bfseries\large,
    title=Glossary (Part 1)
]
\raggedright
\begin{description}
    \item[\textbf{p-value}] The probability of obtaining a result as extreme as the observed one under the null hypothesis.
    \item[\textbf{Detection p-value}] Metric indicating if the probe signal is distinguishable from background noise (values \texttt{>} 0.01 are often unreliable).
    \item[\textbf{Beta-value}] Ratio of methylated to total signal, representing methylation level (range 0–1).
    \item[\textbf{M-value}] Log$_2$ ratio of methylated to unmethylated signal; preferred for statistical testing due to improved variance properties.
    \item[\textbf{CpG site}] DNA region where a cytosine nucleotide is followed by a guanine; key location for DNA methylation.
    \item[\textbf{Heatmap}] A color-coded matrix visualization representing probe methylation across samples, often used for clustering analysis.
    \item[\textbf{Workspace}] The R environment that stores all loaded objects during a session.
    \item[\textbf{Object}] Any data structure stored in R, such as vectors, data frames, or lists.
    \item[\textbf{Function}] A block of R code that performs a specific task.
    \item[\textbf{Variable}] A name assigned to an object in R, enabling later reference.
    \item[\textbf{Script}] A text file (.R) containing a sequence of R commands.
    \item[\textbf{Console}] The interactive R prompt in RStudio or R.
    \item[\textbf{Data frame}] A two-dimensional table-like structure with rows and columns.
    \item[\textbf{Vector}] A one-dimensional sequence of data elements of the same type.
    \item[\textbf{List}] A flexible container that can store multiple types of R objects.
\end{description}
\end{tcolorbox}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\begin{tcolorbox}[
    breakable,
    colback=white,
    colframe=black!80,
    boxrule=0.7pt,
    arc=4pt,
    left=6pt,
    right=6pt,
    top=6pt,
    bottom=6pt,
    fonttitle=\bfseries\large,
    title=Glossary (Part 2)
]
\raggedright
\begin{description}
    \item[\textbf{Loop}] A control structure that repeats a block of code multiple times.
    \item[\textbf{Operator}] A symbol (e.g., \texttt{+}, \texttt{-}, \texttt{>}, \texttt{<}, \texttt{==}) used to perform operations.
    \item[\textbf{False Discovery Rate (FDR)}] Correction method that controls the expected proportion of false positives among significant results.
    \item[\textbf{Dye Bias}] Technical artifact introduced by different dye efficiencies; corrected via normalization.
    \item[\textbf{Manifest file}] Metadata file that maps probe IDs to genomic coordinates and annotations.
    \item[\textbf{RGChannelSet}] minfi object storing raw red and green channel intensities.
    \item[\textbf{MethylSet}] minfi object after background correction, ready for downstream analysis.
    \item[\textbf{Normalization}] Process that adjusts data to remove technical variation while preserving biological differences.
    \item[\textbf{Batch effect}] Unwanted variation introduced by experimental conditions (e.g., processing date, operator).
    \item[\textbf{Pipeline}] An ordered sequence of analysis steps.
    \item[\textbf{Preprocessing}] Steps like background correction and normalization applied to raw data to prepare it for analysis.
    \item[\textbf{Differential methylation}] Statistical comparison of methylation levels between groups (e.g., disease vs. control).
    \item[\textbf{t-test}] A statistical test comparing the means of two groups to identify significant differences.
    \item[\textbf{Volcano plot}] A scatter plot showing significance (–log$_{10}$ p-value) versus effect size (e.g., delta beta).
    \item[\textbf{Manhattan plot}] Genome-wide plot showing –log$_{10}$ p-values by chromosome position.
    \item[\textbf{PCA (Principal Component Analysis)}] A technique to explore sample variance and detect batch effects.
    \item[\textbf{Pipe operator (\texttt{\%>\%})}] An operator from magrittr/dplyr to chain commands for cleaner syntax (not used in this workflow).
\end{description}
\end{tcolorbox}

\section{Theoretical Background}
\subsection*{DNA Methylation}
DNA methylation is an epigenetic modification that occurs when a methyl group is added to the 5' position of cytosine residues within CpG dinucleotides. This modification is crucial for regulating gene expression and maintaining genome stability. Aberrant methylation is associated with diseases such as cancer and neurological disorders.

\subsection*{Illumina 450K BeadChip}
The Illumina HumanMethylation450K BeadChip enables high-throughput analysis of over 485,000 CpG sites across the genome. It uses bisulfite conversion to differentiate between methylated and unmethylated cytosines:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Infinium I}: Two separate probes (one for methylated, one for unmethylated) and single-color detection.
    \item \textbf{Infinium II}: A single probe with two-color detection to distinguish methylation status.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Bisulfite Conversion}
Bisulfite treatment converts unmethylated cytosines into uracils (read as thymines after PCR), while methylated cytosines remain unchanged. This differential conversion allows the array probes to detect methylation states.


\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.3}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{8pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{4cm} p{5.2cm} p{5.2cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Characteristic} & \textbf{Infinium I} & \textbf{Infinium II} \\
\midrule
Number of Probes & Two (one for methylated, one for unmethylated) & One (single probe detects both) \\
Detection Channel & Single color per probe & Dual color within probe \\
CpG Coverage & Slightly lower & Slightly higher \\
Probe Design & Two separate bead types & One bead type \\
Signal Intensity & Higher dynamic range & Lower dynamic range \\
Normalization & Less dye bias, easier normalization & More dye bias, needs adjustment \\
Dye Bias Susceptibility & Lower & Higher \\
Interpretation & Raw signals easier to interpret & Requires correction for dye bias \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Comparison of Infinium I and Infinium II chemistries on the Illumina 450K array.}
\end{table}


