\section{Pipeline Overview}
An expanded step-by-step pipeline:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Data Import}: Read raw .idat files using \texttt{read.metharray.exp()} to create an \texttt{RGChannelSet}.
    \item \textbf{Annotation}: Use the cleaned manifest file (\texttt{Illumina450Manifest\_clean.RData}) to map probe IDs and design types.
    \item \textbf{Quality Control}: Calculate detection p-values (\texttt{detectionP()}) and generate quality control plots (\texttt{plotQC()}, \texttt{controlStripPlot()}).
    \item \textbf{Preprocessing}: Apply background correction with \texttt{preprocessRaw()} and functional normalization with \texttt{preprocessFunnorm()}.
    \item \textbf{Beta/M-value Calculation}: Calculate Beta-values (\texttt{getBeta()}) and M-values (\texttt{getM()}).
    \item \textbf{Statistical Testing}: Perform t-tests (\texttt{t.test()}) comparing groups.
    \item \textbf{Multiple Testing Correction}: Apply correction for multiple testing using \texttt{p.adjust()} (methods: BH, Bonferroni).
    \item \textbf{Visualization}: Generate density plots, boxplots, PCA plots (\texttt{prcomp()}, \texttt{fviz\_eig()}), volcano plots, Manhattan plots (\texttt{qqman}), and heatmaps (\texttt{heatmap.2()}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{The Analytical Pipeline — From Biological Question to Visualization}
\noindent
A successful DNA methylation study requires a well-designed workflow that systematically addresses every analytical step. Below, we outline a typical pipeline, emphasizing its logical flow from hypothesis formulation to data visualization:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Biological Question}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Define the scientific goal, e.g., “Does methylation differ between disease and control groups?”
        \item Establish hypotheses and experimental design (number of samples, replicates, conditions).
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Sample Collection and Preparation}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Extract DNA from relevant tissues or cell types.
        \item Perform bisulfite conversion to differentiate methylated from unmethylated cytosines.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Data Acquisition}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Use the Illumina HumanMethylation450K array (or EPIC) to profile methylation.
        \item Generate raw data files (.idat) containing red and green channel intensities.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Data Import into R}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Use \texttt{read.metharray.exp()} to read .idat files into an \texttt{RGChannelSet} object.
        \item Inspect sample metadata (e.g., group, sex, batch) from \texttt{SampleSheet.csv}.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Annotation}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Link probe IDs to genomic features using the cleaned manifest (\texttt{Illumina450Manifest
        \_clean.RData}).
        \item Exclude problematic probes (e.g., SNP-overlapping, cross-hybridizing).
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Quality Control}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Calculate detection p-values with \texttt{detectionP()} to filter unreliable probes.
        \item Assess sample quality using \texttt{plotQC()} and \texttt{controlStripPlot()} for negative controls.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Preprocessing}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Apply background correction using \texttt{preprocessRaw()}.
        \item Normalize data using functional normalization with \texttt{preprocessFunnorm()} to mitigate technical biases and batch effects.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Methylation Quantification}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Calculate Beta-values with \texttt{getBeta()} and M-values with \texttt{getM()}.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Statistical Testing}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Perform t-tests for group comparisons (\texttt{t.test()}).
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Multiple Testing Correction}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Correct p-values for multiple testing using \texttt{p.adjust()} (methods: BH and Bonferroni).
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Visualization}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Generate density plots, boxplots, PCA plots (\texttt{prcomp()}, \texttt{fviz\_eig()}), volcano plots, Manhattan plots (\texttt{qqman}), and heatmaps (\texttt{heatmap.2()}).
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Biological Interpretation}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Map significant CpGs to genes and pathways.
        \item Integrate methylation findings with external datasets (e.g., gene expression, clinical data).
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

\noindent
This pipeline illustrates how each step builds upon the previous one, transforming raw experimental data into biologically meaningful insights.



